A minimal windowing pattern (the core idea behind libraries like `react-window` or `TanStack Virtual`), showing what's actually kept in the DOM:

```jsx
function VirtualList({ items, rowHeight, viewportHeight }) {
  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);

  const startIndex = Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight);
  const visibleCount = Math.ceil(viewportHeight / rowHeight) + 2; // +buffer rows
  const visibleItems = items.slice(startIndex, startIndex + visibleCount);

  return (
    <div onScroll={(e) => setScrollTop(e.target.scrollTop)} style={{ height: viewportHeight, overflow: "auto" }}>
      <div style={{ height: items.length * rowHeight, position: "relative" }}>
        {visibleItems.map((item, i) => (
          <div key={item.id} style={{ position: "absolute", top: (startIndex + i) * rowHeight }}>
            {item.label}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

The outer div's height (`items.length * rowHeight`) gives the scrollbar the correct total size, while the inner absolutely-positioned rows are only ever the ~17 or so actually visible — `visibleItems`, not `items`, is what gets mapped to real DOM nodes.
